speechtext:
I saw an announcement in the press the other day with regard to the Naval College at Halifax which, if true, should be made to the House. I would like to ask the Minister of Naval Affairs what changes the department proposes to make in regard to the future operation of that college, the basis of the changes, and whether or not the college is to be con, tinued.
Mr: HAZEN: The regulations have been laid on the table of the House and have been published in the Canada Gazette in full;

more clearly and briefly, perhaps, than I could give the information.

They say, Oh, no;
but I know the history of this question. I have been as long in this House as any of these hon. gentlemen, and I know the fights that came up year after year and how many fights we had to try and get an entrance into the city of Toronto from the rural sections. There is no doubt about it that the people of the counties outside of Toronto claim that they have a right- I would say a vested right-to get into the city of Toronto. They have helped to build up Toronto. If it had not been for the people of the adjacent counties the city of Toronto would be a small town. The people of Toronto are indebted to the people of the country for the size of their city and the sooner they permit the people in the counties lying east and west to enjoy the privileges which they are by nature entitled to have the better for the city of Toronto. If they do not want our fresh eggs and prefer eating stale eggs, of course, they can shut us out, but they can have their choice. We prefer the fresh eggs, and we will use them. We have an abundance of them. We can supply the people of Toronto every day with all they want and more than they need if they will only let us in. But, unfortunately, they made a bad contract about twenty-five years ago with a company, giving them a franchise over the streets of Toronto to last for thirty years. That contract will not expire for six or seven years yet, and the people of Toronto are afraid that whoever owns this franchise in Toronto will claim heavy damages if any other electric road is permitted to get into the city and pass over its streets. Consequently these gentlemen are fighting, not for the city of Toronto, but to protect themselves against the penalties that would be imposed upon them if* this road were permitted to get in. I hope and trust that very soon they will be willing to give up the fight, that they will see eye to eye with us and will be willing to let these outside roads find a way in. After they had imposed onerous conditions on the road coming in from the east, another application was made for the Hamilton and Guelph Railway Company-I am not sure whether that is the name or not, as there are so many of them-and these conditions were imposed so onerously that that company was prohibited from getting in. I do not want to talk the Bill out, but I want to get a resolution passed that will repeal clause 7, and then I am willing that the Bill should go through. The Toronto and suburban road is being constructed now through my county from one side of it to 207
the other. It is graded, everything is ready practically to lay down the ties, and then we will have a road running from Guelph to Toronto if we can only get into Toronto. But the people of Toronto play-I was going to say hog, but I shall not use that term-they are very selfish, they say: No, we will not let you in; we have a railway in Toronto that claims the franchise and if we let any other railway in we will have to pay a penalty to Sir William Mackenzie; we do not want to fight him, but we would rather fight the people of the county of Hal-ton. Consequently, they are putting a clause in this Bill that makes it impossible for this company to gat in. I am told as a fact that when this clause found its way into the Bill three clauses were included in one motion. Had they been moved separately I do not believe this clause would ever have gone into the Bill but this was a shrewd plan adopted by those who were promoting the Bill to get this clause in. Consequently, they moved two clauses quite inoffensive and acceptable and a third clause very offensive and not acceptable and all went through together. The result is that the clause as it appears here practically shuts this road out of Toronto as long as this Act remains on the statute book. I appeal to the members of this House to be fair to the outlying counties and give the people the right to get into this big market which I contend the rural sections have created. I am quite sure that the people of Toronto will be well satisfied when they come to find the great advantage that will accrue to them from the operation of this road.
